  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =12x-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =12-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =12(1-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12(1+x)(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(1+x)(1-x) & >0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-1) & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)<0$ when $-1<x<1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)>0,$ which has the solutions $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
